# Time: now, durations, calendar days and the dev clock

A `time` is Unix milliseconds in UTC, and `now` is the writing host's clock.
Durations are milliseconds too; `day`, `hour`, `bucket`, `month` and `year` turn a time into a calendar number.

```quarry
use personas alice, bob

table shifts:
  owner: principal
  title: text 1..80
  startsAt: time                          -- Unix milliseconds, UTC
  endsAt: time
  createdAt: time default now             -- stamped by the writing host's clock
  immutable owner
  read <- .owner = viewer
  insert <- .owner = viewer
  update <- .owner = viewer
  delete <- .owner = viewer
  cap 500 by owner
  sync to .owner

view shiftsByDay: count shifts by .owner, day(.startsAt)      -- a UTC day number per group
  read <- .owner = viewer

table checkins:
  person: principal
  at: time default now
  unique person, day(at) else ALREADY_CHECKED_IN 'One check-in per day.'
  read <- .person = viewer
  insert <- .person = viewer
  sync to .person last 30 by .at

query upcoming(c: cursor ?):
  return shifts where .owner = viewer and .startsAt >= now first 20 by .startsAt after c

query today():                            -- day: 20737; midnight: 1791676800000 (2026-10-11T00:00Z)
  return { day: day(now), midnight: day(now) * 1d, shifts: shiftsByDay[viewer, day(now)] }

query pacificToday():                     -- days that start at 08:00 UTC: midnight at UTC-8, no DST
  return { day: day(now, 8h), starts: day(now, 8h) * 1d + 8h }

query thisMonth():                        -- month: 681 (2026-10), year: 2026; [start, end) as times
  m = month(now)
  return { month: m, year: year(now), start: monthStart(m), end: monthStart(m + 1) }

mutation addShift(title: text 1..80, startsAt: time, hours: int):
  require hours >= 1 and hours <= 24 else BAD_LENGTH 'A shift is 1 to 24 hours.'
  require startsAt >= now - 1d else IN_THE_PAST
  return insert shifts { owner: viewer, title, startsAt, endsAt: startsAt + hours * 1h }

mutation checkIn():
  return insert checkins { person: viewer }
```

## Values

- `time` columns and arguments hold Unix milliseconds (UTC). On the wire and in JS a time is a number:
  `Date.now()`, `new Date(row.startsAt)`. Fixture rows use the same numbers.
- `now` is the clock of the host running the operation: the server, or the device for a predicted write.
  `default now` stamps an omitted field the same way.
- Duration grammar: one non-negative integer immediately followed by `ms`, `s`, `m`, `h` or `d`; no spaces, fractions or compound `1h30m` literals. Use `1h + 30m` in Quarry.
- Duration literals: `500ms`, `30s`, `15m`, `24h`, `7d`. They are plain integers of milliseconds:
  `now + 1h`, `.endsAt - .startsAt` (elapsed ms), `hours * 1h`, `.at >= now - 7d`.
- There are no time zones and no date type. Compute local wall-clock dates, weekdays and local months in
  the client from the millisecond number; UTC months are `month(t)` below.

## Calendar numbers: day, hour, bucket

- `day(t)` is the UTC day number, `floor(t / 1d)`: 20737 is 2026-10-11, not a timestamp.
  `day(t) * 1d` is that day's midnight UTC as a time; in JS `new Date(day * 86_400_000)`.
- `hour(t)` counts **hours since the epoch**, not the hour on a clock.
  `hourOfDay(t)` returns the UTC clock hour 0..23, including before 1970.
  `bucket(t, 15m)` counts any period. At 2026-10-11 17:30 UTC,
  `hour(t)` is 497705 and `hourOfDay(t)` is 17.
- An offset moves the boundary: `day(t, 8h)` days start at 08:00 UTC (midnight at UTC-8, without DST),
  `hour(t, 30m)`, `bucket(t, 1d, 8h)`. The offset is fixed, so a zone with DST shifts by an hour.
- Use them as group keys (`view … by .owner, day(.startsAt)`), in `unique person, day(at)` (one per day),
  and in range filters (`.at >= day(now) * 1d` is "since midnight UTC").
- A `time` column accepts any integer, so storing `day(now)` there type-checks and reads as January 1970.
  Store `day(now) * 1d`, or keep the day number in an `int` column.

```quarry
query clock(t: time):
  return {ordinal: hour(t), clock: hourOfDay(t)}
```

## Months and years (UTC)

- `month(t)` counts months since 1970-01: 681 is 2026-10, -1 is 1969-12. Arithmetic is continuous:
  `month(now) - 1` is last month, `month(.at) >= month(now) - 2` is the last three months.
- `year(t)` is the calendar year (2026); `month(t) / 12 + 1970` equals it.
- `monthStart(m)` and `yearStart(y)` are the first instants, as `time`s. A month is `[monthStart(m),
  monthStart(m + 1))`; there is no `monthEnd`, because ranges are half-open. They refuse E_OVERFLOW only
  beyond about ±285,000 years.
- Keys and views take them like `day`: `unique person, month(at) else ALREADY_THIS_MONTH`,
  `view … by .owner, month(.at)`, `checkins[viewer, month(now)]`.
- In JS: `Date.UTC(1970, m)` is the month's start; `(d.getUTCFullYear() - 1970) * 12 + d.getUTCMonth()`
  is the key. A JS `Date` spans ±8.64e15 ms, less than a `time`, so the extreme times are `Invalid Date`.
- Months and years are UTC; they take no offset. Local-time months (a time zone) are not available:
  for a fixed-offset zone west of UTC, store the client's local month in an `int` column.

## Schedules and expiry

- `schedule nightly every 1d at 03:00: digest` fires once per period of the server's clock, in UTC;
  periods are `every 2m`, `1h` or `1d [at HH:MM]` (`guide jobs`).
- `schedule billing every 1 month at day 1 09:00: bill` fires on calendar months (UTC); `every 3 months`
  fires in January, April, July and October, `every 1 year` in January. Days 1–28 only.
  `month(periodStart)` is the month that just ended.
- `expire at .expiresAt` ends a row's life at a time; a null time never expires (`guide expire`).
- `not before .opensAt` starts a server-enforced read lifetime when the server's
  durable clock reaches the value; null is immediately readable. Device wall
  clocks and server-time estimates never authorize early disclosure. The
  compiled `[opensAt, expiresAt)` recipe is `guide timed disclosure`.

## E_TIME_AUDIENCE_PENDING

E_TIME_AUDIENCE_PENDING means an opened row is still waiting for its bounded server publication. A read
never decides that the row is absent. HTTP queries wait for encountered live
publication dependencies, then reevaluate; a 30-second limit returns retryable
E_QUERY_RETRY (503). Other unavailable reasons can still return loading. A mutation rolls
back without retaining its operation id, so the client can retry the same
write. The refusal is retryable and reveals no row id or opening time.

- Catch-up: a schedule fires once per boundary, never twice. Boundaries it missed (the server was
  down, or `clock advance` jumped past them) coalesce into one run for the latest period once
  the server is back; `periodStart`/`periodEnd` describe that last period only. A schedule
  first seen fires from its next boundary.
- So a job that sweeps exactly `[periodStart, periodEnd)` never visits rows that fell due in the
  skipped periods. Sweep back from `periodEnd` and skip rows already handled, so one run catches up:

```quarry
table trains:
  name: text <=40
  departsAt: time
  open: bool default true
  immutable departsAt
  public 'the departures board'
  insert <- allow
  online only

job closeDeparted(periodStart: time, periodEnd: time):
  for t in trains where .departsAt >= periodEnd - 7d and .departsAt < periodEnd by .departsAt:
    if t.open:
      update trains[t.id] { open: false }

schedule closer every 1m: closeDeparted
```

- `N_SWEEP_DEADLINE` names a timed scheduled status update and a mutation
  testing that status, or testing/writing a flag also tested by the job, without
  a direct comparison on that row deadline. If this is an operation deadline,
  enforce its boundary in the mutation; choose the opening or
  closing direction and equality explicitly. The syntactic note can miss helper
  paths, correlate unrelated rows/branches or warn when a helper guards time;
  it proves no safety. Notification-only sweeps need no extra deadline guard.
- Where the decision is made by a caller, compare with `now` instead and need no job:
  `require t.departsAt > now else DEPARTED`. `guide recipes --section schedules` has both together.
- A period you still need runs by hand: `snapback4 run closeDeparted '{"periodStart":…,"periodEnd":…}'`.

## The dev clock

Clock replies include sessionNotice: large jumps expire ordinary seven-day
password sessions and collect unplaced uploads after one hour. Sign in again
and upload after the jump. Reset replaces accounts and sessions: discard cached
credentials/localStorage, sign up and reprovision. Password-only dev requires
`--unsafe-dev-doors`; shared real-user tests use the MIG staging clock above.


- CLI clock durations use one positive whole number and `ms`, `s`, `m`, `h`, `d` or `w`, optionally prefixed `+`: use `90m`, not `1h30m`. Quarry uses `7d` instead of `1w`.
- Classified staging uses `serve <dir> --clock-offset +25h` and `clock advance 2h --release <dir>` with the same duration and drain semantics. `startServe(dir, {clockOffset: "+25h"})` exposes `now()` and `advance("2h")`; both use local operator authority. Production, legacy, account and read-only sessions refuse clock control. Offset is process-local; restart drops it, while persisted disclosure floors never retreat. Ordinary user sessions retain their normal expiry; sign in again after large advances. Staging clock control admits no persona, fixture, reset or dev-job authority.
- `snapback4 dev --clock-offset +25h` starts the dev server 25 hours ahead; mutations stamp `now` from it.
- `snapback4 clock advance 2m [--port N]` moves a running dev forward, sweeps expiry and runs due
  schedules and jobs; over HTTP: `POST /dev/clock/advance` with `{"duration":"2m"}`. An advance across
  many boundaries runs each schedule once, and its reply lists firedWindows and
  skippedBoundaries with periodStart/periodEnd and up to 32 literal replay commands
  per schedule; skippedPeriods is exact and skippedBoundariesOmitted counts the rest. Add --each-boundary (HTTP eachBoundary:true) to admit every crossed
  window in chronological order. Jobs see final advanced now; use period arguments
  for historical windows, or separate advances to test now at each boundary.
  Only --each-boundary refuses more than 10000 total crossed windows before changing time;
  coalesced advances have no crossed-window cap. Windows are selected at the sampled
  advance time; time spent draining maintenance cannot invent additional fired windows.
  If work fails after time advances, inspect now/skewMs; normal maintenance resumes
  queued work. Reset the scenario with reset --clock reset rather than repeating
  the advance blindly. E_CLOCK_INTERRUPTED means shutdown stopped the drain:
  time already moved; inspect queued jobs before restarting the test.
- Clock and reset discover a unique live dev in the current project, including `dev --memory --port N`. Multiple hosts list their addresses and require `--port N`; no default port is guessed. Registrations live in `.snapback4/dev-hosts`, contain no secrets, and stale hosts are ignored.
- The offset belongs to the process: restart with `dev --resume-clock` to continue from the store's
  newest stamp. `GET /dev/status` reports `now`, `skewMs` and `clockOffsetMs`. `/dev/clock/status` and reset
  also carry now/skewMs; `await dev.now()` from snapback4/proof reads it afresh.
  now/skewMs include a persisted timed-disclosure floor, which may hold served time
  after restart; --resume-clock continues the store’s advanced clock. clockOffsetMs
  and offsetMs report the exact configured process offset, unchanged on refusal.
- `snapback4 run <op> --now +2h` (or milliseconds) pins the clock for one local run.
- In the client, `client.now()` is the server's time estimate; `useNow(deadline)` from `snapback4/react`
  re-renders at a deadline.

## Monthly billing (UTC) with `money`

One invoice per subscription per calendar month, billed by a monthly
schedule. `month(t)` is the month ordinal (681 is 2026-10), so the months to
bill are plain ints and `unique subscription, month` makes each invoice once.

```quarry
table plans:
  name: text 1..40
  price: money USD
  unique name
  public 'the price list'
  insert <- deny
  cap 20
  sync public

table subscriptions:
  owner: principal
  plan: plans
  startMonth: int
  billedThrough: int                     -- the last month invoiced
  immutable owner, plan, startMonth
  read <- .owner = viewer
  insert <- .owner = viewer
  update <- deny
  online only

table invoices:
  subscription: subscriptions
  owner: principal
  month: int
  amount: money USD
  unique subscription, month
  immutable subscription, owner, month, amount
  read <- .owner = viewer
  insert <- deny
  update <- deny
  delete <- deny
  online only

mutation subscribe(plan: plans):
  require exists plans[plan] else NOT_FOUND
  m = month(now)
  return insert subscriptions { owner: viewer, plan, startMonth: m, billedThrough: m - 1 }

query myInvoices(c: cursor ?):
  return invoices where .owner = viewer last 12 by .month after c

job bill(periodStart: time, periodEnd: time):
  due = month(periodStart)                -- the month that just ended
  for s in subscriptions:
    p = plans[s.plan] ! else NOT_FOUND
    upto = min(due, s.billedThrough + 12)  -- at most twelve months per subscription per run
    if s.billedThrough < upto:
      for m in range(s.billedThrough + 1, upto + 1):
        insert invoices { subscription: s.id, owner: s.owner, month: m, amount: p.price } if absent
      update subscriptions[s.id] { billedThrough: upto }

schedule billing every 1 month at day 1 09:00: bill
```

The schedule fires at 09:00 UTC on the 1st. Missed boundaries coalesce into
one run, so the job bills from `billedThrough`, not just `periodStart`'s
month: after a two-month outage the next run bills both months, and a rerun
writes nothing (`if absent`, then `billedThrough` has moved). A backlog
longer than twelve months shrinks by eleven a run. Charge a payment provider
from an `effect` keyed by the invoice id, the provider's idempotency key;
the unique invoice stops duplicate rows, not duplicate charges. Test with
`snapback4 clock advance 62d` on a running `dev`. Months here are UTC; a
local-time month needs a time zone, which Quarry does not have.
# Staging with real users

Staging runs real password users and application rules under serve. Use it for shared previews and probe:serve, with roles provisioned by a local operator.

```quarry
use identity password
table roles:
  principal: principal
  role: enum('admin', 'member')
  unique principal
  immutable principal
  read <- .principal = viewer or viewer = 'service:operator'
  insert <- viewer = 'service:operator'
  update <- viewer = 'service:operator'
  online only
mutation provision(principal: principal, role: enum('admin', 'member')):
  require viewer = 'service:operator' else NOT_OPERATOR
  return upsert roles[principal] { principal, role }
query myRole():
  return roles[viewer]
table notes:
  owner: principal
  body: text 1..200
  read <- .owner = viewer
  insert <- .owner = viewer
  online only
mutation add(body: text 1..200):
  return insert notes { owner: viewer, body }
query myNotes(c: cursor ?):
  return notes where .owner = viewer first 20 by .id after c
```

Use `serve` for staging, shared previews and `probe:serve`. It enforces the same
password identity and rules as production. `dev` always advertises
`development:true` in `/auth/config`, even with closed doors; probes should
refuse it. `guide deploy` covers release evolution; PROD2 owns starter adaptation.

## Password roles and operator provisioning

Save this complete source as `snapback/schema.q` in an app with snapback4 installed.
Signup creates an account without a role. Only the local filesystem operator can
bootstrap this roster. The insert rule enforces the boundary independently of
the mutation. SEC owns self-promotion diagnostics and invariant proofs.

## Stand up staging

Run `npx snapback4 check` to generate the API. Save this as `staging.mjs` next to
snapback/, then run `node staging.mjs`. Use `node staging.mjs --once` for CI setup
and shutdown. The deployment and private credentials file remain for inspection;
choose fresh names for each independent run. Add both to .gitignore. PORT_LO and
PORT_HI optionally choose a port range. The printed URL belongs to `serve`.

```js
import { openSync, writeFileSync, closeSync } from "node:fs";
import { deploy, startServe } from "snapback4/proof";
import { api } from "./snapback/generated/api.ts";

await deploy("staging", "snapback", { args: ["--staging"] });
const host = await startServe("staging", { seedUsers: {
  roles: { admin: 2, member: 2 }, provision: api.provision,
} });
try {
  // This file contains real passwords and bearer tokens. Never commit it.
  const fd = openSync("staging-users.json", "wx", 0o600);
  try { writeFileSync(fd, JSON.stringify(host.sessions)); } finally { closeSync(fd); }
  for (const accounts of Object.values(host.sessions)) {
    for (const account of accounts) {
      const response = await fetch(host.url + "/m/add", {
        method: "POST", headers: { "content-type": "application/json", authorization: `Bearer ${account.token}` },
        body: JSON.stringify({ args: { body: `Welcome ${account.role}` } }),
        signal: AbortSignal.timeout(10000),
      });
      if ((await response.json()).state !== "sent") throw Error("staging data write refused");
    }
  }
  console.log(JSON.stringify({ backendUrl: host.url, credentialsFile: "staging-users.json" }));
  if (!process.argv.includes("--once")) await new Promise(resolve => {
    process.once("SIGTERM", resolve); process.once("SIGINT", resolve);
  });
} finally { await host.stop(); }
```

`host.seedAccounts({roles, provision, args?})` performs additional batches; an
optional `args(account)` merges over `{principal, role}` (explicit keys win).
Use `service: "ops"` for a provisioning mutation authorized as `service:ops`.
Each call creates new random email/password pairs and returns role arrays of
`{role,email,password,principal,token,expiresAt}`. Ordinary sessions last seven
days; sign in again with `login` from snapback4/auth when needed. Setup is
sequential and bounded (default five minutes, timeoutMs overrides it, max 100
accounts). Only explicit identity throttling refusals retry; ambiguous writes
do not. Partial failure can leave accounts/grants: inspect or discard this
disposable deployment before retrying. Returned credentials never enter helper
logs. Print them only to a private operator destination, as above.

## Production refuses bulk setup

`deploy --staging` can classify only a new store. Existing unmarked stores are
production, and refuse relabelling. Classification stays with the store across
hot deploys, rollbacks and restarts; copying production into a directory named
staging does not change it. `deploy --check --staging` does not mark a store.
There is no promotion flag: deploy real production into its own fresh directory
and provision only intended users. `seedAccounts` and startServe's seedUsers
refuse production or dev before minting a token or creating any account. A
normal production startServe still supports operatorToken for explicit grants.

### E_STAGING

E_STAGING refuses production relabelling or bulk account setup on a non-staging
host. Choose a separate fresh `deploy --staging` store; use the explicit
first-admin recipe below for intended production users. Source edits and a
directory named staging never change a production store's classification.


### Account setup refusals

E_STAGING_INPUT means the account plan is invalid. Provide 1..100 accounts,
named roles, a declared mutation handle and a positive timeoutMs before setup.

E_STAGING_TIMEOUT means the setup deadline expired. Partial accounts/grants
may exist; inspect or discard the disposable deployment before retrying.

E_STAGING_SIGNUP means password signup refused or returned an invalid session.
Inspect the underlying refusal; partial accounts/grants may exist, so inspect
or discard the disposable deployment before retrying.

E_STAGING_PROVISION means the operator mutation refused or returned an invalid
reply. Repair its arguments and operator-only rules; partial accounts/grants
may exist, so inspect or discard the disposable deployment before retrying.

## First admin on production

Deploy the same schema with `npx snapback4 deploy production` (omit --staging).
Save the following as `first-admin.mjs`. Set FIRST_ADMIN_EMAIL and
FIRST_ADMIN_PASSWORD privately, then run `node first-admin.mjs` as the store
owner while no other serve owns its port. This script starts and stops serve;
restart it normally afterwards. For an already running deployment use
`snapback4 token --service operator --days 1 --release production` locally and
the same authenticated mutation. Keep the service token out of the frontend.

```js
import { signup, login } from "snapback4/auth";
import { startServe } from "snapback4/proof";
const email = process.env.FIRST_ADMIN_EMAIL, password = process.env.FIRST_ADMIN_PASSWORD;
if (!email || !password) throw Error("Set FIRST_ADMIN_EMAIL and FIRST_ADMIN_PASSWORD privately");
const host = await startServe("production");
try {
  // Login first so rerunning this script preserves the existing account and grant.
  let account = await login(host.url, email, password);
  if (!account.ok) account = await signup(host.url, email, password);
  if (!account.ok) throw Error(account.why.message);
  const token = await host.operatorToken();
  const response = await fetch(host.url + "/m/provision", {
    method: "POST", headers: { "content-type": "application/json", authorization: `Bearer ${token}` },
    body: JSON.stringify({ args: { principal: account.session.principal, role: "admin" } }),
    signal: AbortSignal.timeout(10000),
  });
  if ((await response.json()).state !== "sent") throw Error("operator grant refused");
  console.log("First admin provisioned; sign in with the chosen email and password");
} finally { await host.stop(); }
```

Reruns authenticate the same email/password and upsert the same roster row.
A wrong password refuses without changing an existing grant. No first-user race or self-promotion mutation is needed. Subsequent grants use
the same operator process or an explicitly authorized admin policy. Invite codes
must be server-validated, single-use grants issued by an authorized operator;
an arbitrary caller-supplied code or role is not authority.

## Local fixture tools are explicit

Password-only dev closes /seed, /dev/* and persona headers by default.
`dev --unsafe-dev-doors` explicitly opens local fixture and clock tools and
prints a warning; it still requires loopback Host/Origin, and personas must be
declared with `use personas`. The local token-mint protocol keeps its existing
private store-owner proof. Serve never opens these development tools.
`/auth/config` reports `environment`, `development`, and `devDoors` separately.
`startDev()` persists by default, matching native dev; pass args:["--memory"]
for disposable storage. now() needs the explicit clock door on password-only
dev. A proof for real users uses the serve recipe above.

